+# Daily Grind
+
+Requires Node.js 20 or newer. `npm start` serves the homepage and its newsletter endpoint on port 11222; override with `PORT` and `HOST` as needed. No build or dependencies are required.
+
+In production, terminate HTTPS at your reverse proxy and set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the site's public origin (for example, `https://dailygrind.example`) so signup Origin checks work behind the proxy. Preserve the Node server's security response headers.
+
+Signups POST to `/api/newsletter` and are appended to `data/newsletter.jsonl`. Set `DATA_DIR` to a private persistent directory when deploying. The server only serves explicitly listed assets, so subscriber records are never public. This collects newsletter subscriptions; newsletter delivery is a separate process.
+
+Every response includes an enforced `Connection-Allowlist` permitting only the response origin and `https://images.unsplash.com`, with redirects and WebRTC blocked by default. CSP provides a fallback: requests and form submissions stay on the same origin, only images can load from Unsplash, and workers and frames are disabled. The existing inline CSS is now a local stylesheet; only the nonce-authorized newsletter script can run.
+
+`/api/security-reports` accepts bounded Reporting API batches and logs only recognized violation counts, discarding URLs and other report contents. Browsers without Connection-Allowlist support enforce CSP but cannot provide the same network-wide protection for navigation, resource hints, or WebRTC. See the [Connection Allowlists specification](https://wicg.github.io/connection-allowlists/).
+
+The form has native email validation and a non-JavaScript POST fallback. JavaScript handles rejected HTTP responses, policy/network failures, and a 15-second timeout with an accessible retry message.
